Yes. For any given $p \ge 1$, choose any $q > 2p+1$; then $\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^p\sqrt{a_n}$ converges.

**Proof.**

By hypothesis, $\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^q a_n$ converges for every $q$; in particular we may pick $q$ with $q > 2p+1$.

Write
$$
n^p\sqrt{a_n} \;=\; n^{p-q/2}\cdot n^{q/2}\sqrt{a_n} \;=\; n^{p-q/2}\sqrt{\,n^q a_n\,}.
$$
Applying the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality to the two nonnegative sequences $u_n = n^{p-q/2}$ and $v_n = \sqrt{n^q a_n}$,
$$
\left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^p\sqrt{a_n}\right)^{\!2}
= \left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty u_n v_n\right)^{\!2}
\le \left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty u_n^2\right)\!\left(\sum_{n=1}^\infty v_n^2\right).
$$

Now
$$
\sum_{n=1}^\infty u_n^2 = \sum_{n=1}^\infty n^{2p-q},
\qquad
\sum_{n=1}^\infty v_n^2 = \sum_{n=1}^\infty n^q a_n.
$$

- Since $q > 2p+1$, we have $2p - q < -1$, so the $p$-series $\sum n^{2p-q}$ converges.
- By hypothesis, $\sum n^q a_n$ converges.

Both factors on the right-hand side are finite, hence $\left(\sum n^p\sqrt{a_n}\right)^2 < \infty$, and therefore $\sum_{n=1}^\infty n^p\sqrt{a_n}$ converges. $\blacksquare$

